WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:06.700
What's going on everybody and welcome to a Python Plays GTA V tutorial series.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:08.740
So in this series, what we're going to be doing is

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:13.020
just simply exploring the open world of Grand Theft Auto with Python,

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:15.520
seeing what we can come up with and what we can do.

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:17.440
The truth is I really don't know.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:22.000
The only thing I've done for this entire series is just make sure it's possible.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.800
So I wanted to make certain a few things.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:27.940
One thing is that we can just process frames quickly enough,

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:31.780
so if we can only process frames at like 1 or 2 frames a second,

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:36.560
that's probably not gonna be good enough to really do much at all in this game.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:42.780
So I was really kinda hoping for at least a 10+ frame per second process rate,

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:45.700
especially if all we're doing is trying is just trying to get the frames into Python.

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:49.440
Coz then we're gonna in theory we'll to be running algorithms and all kinds of stuff.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:53.880
So that was one thing I wanted to confirm and the other thing I wanted to confirm was that

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:54.420
we can actually control Grand Theft Auto with Python.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:54.920
I was pretty sure we could just use Zenkeys or PyAutoGui, but I wasn't positive

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:55.420
on that.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:57.700
As long as we can do those two things, I knew that

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:07.460
"Okay, we can actually do this project."

